A '''keiretsu''' ({{nihongo|系列|keiretsu}}, "system" or "lineage") is a group of Sanese companies bound together by cross-shareholdings, interlocking directorates, long-term supplier relationships, and a shared primary lender. The keiretsu is the organizational backbone of [[Guided Capitalism]], the state-guided economic model under which Okaiken industrialized, and the six major groups — the horizontal, bank-centred [[Sangoku Group]], [[Aizawa Consortium]], [[Mitoyo Holdings]] and [[Okami Financial Group]], and the vertical, manufacturer-centred [[Yonezawa Motors]] and [[Kizan Systems]] — still organize the industrial core of the [[Economy of Okaiken|Sanese economy]], together accounting for roughly the industrial sector's 27.4% share of GDP. All six are headquartered in the capital, [[Okami]], and coordinate their positions through the [[Keiretsu Council]] (経団連), the federation that forms, with the ministries and the [[Bank of Okaiken]], the private pillar of what Sanese political scientists call the {{nihongo|"iron triangle"|鉄の三角形|Tetsu no Sankakukei}}.

The keiretsu emerged in the early 1950s from the remains of the pre-war [[zaibatsu]], the family-owned conglomerates that had driven Sanese industrialization and grown rich on the [[Great Adonian War]]. The [[Armistice of Okami]] of 28 April 1952, which ended the war without occupation and with Sanese sovereignty intact, required the Sanese government to dissolve the zaibatsu as concentrations of war-profiteering power. The implementing legislation of Prime Minister Munenori Mitsui's government destroyed the zaibatsu's legal form — the family holding-company pyramid — but left the groups' banks, managerial cadres, and supplier networks intact. Between 1952 and the mid-1950s the houses reconstituted themselves as keiretsu: cross-shareholdings replaced the family apex, presidents' councils replaced the patriarchs, and the family banks were reborn as the system's main banks. The reorganization was substantially complete by 1953, the year the reorganized Matsudaira group took the neutral name {{nihongo|Sangoku|三國|"three lands"}} and the four horizontal groups founded the Keiretsu Council.

As the engine of the [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] of 1955–1975, the system coordinated investment across entire supply chains, absorbed shocks through internal cross-subsidization, and aligned banks, manufacturers, and suppliers around the industrial priorities of the [[Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] (MITI) and the state-owned [[Okaiken Development Bank]]. The same structures later proved resistant to correction. In the [[Go-go 8090]] bubble of the 1980s the main banks lent against inflated land collateral, and when the bubble burst the cross-shareholdings that had insulated members from market pressure prevented the recognition of losses, producing the {{nihongo|zombie banks|ゾンビ銀行|Zonbi Ginkō}} and {{nihongo|zombie enterprises|ゾンビ企業|Zonbi Kigyō}} of the [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decade]].

The system has been under sustained assault since the 2010s. The Communist government of Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]] (2016–2021) denounced the groups as {{nihongo|parasitic monopolies|寄生虫的独占|Kiseichū-teki Dokusen}} and rewrote corporate taxation squarely against them; the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]] scandal of 2020–2024 revealed that the oldest keiretsu bank had concealed some 円180 billion in bad loans through off-balance-sheet vehicles; and the [[Banking Transparency Act]] of 2024 now requires the banks at the system's core to unwind the cross-shareholdings that have underpinned it for seven decades, on a five-year timetable running to 2029. At the same time [[Kizan Systems]] — a state-seeded semiconductor group with no main bank, no cross-shareholding web, and no presidents' council of its own — has become both the archetype of a post-keiretsu industrial organization and, to its critics, evidence that the new model reproduces the old dependencies in new form.

Whether the keiretsu is therefore in terminal decline or in the middle of its third reinvention — from zaibatsu to keiretsu to whatever the 2020s are producing — remains the central dispute of Sanese political economy.

==Etymology and usage==
The word {{nihongo|keiretsu|系列}} literally denotes a "system", "series", or "lineage" and carries no corporate meaning in ordinary Sanese. Its financial application is a post-war coinage of journalism and official statistics that stabilized in the 1960s and 1970s, when the reorganized groups needed a name that was not "zaibatsu" — the armistice settlement had made the older word a legal category of dissolution. Two structural types are distinguished. Horizontal keiretsu are bank-centred constellations of companies in unrelated industries, bound by cross-shareholdings and a common main bank; three of the four descend directly from the dissolved zaibatsu, and the fourth, the Okami Financial Group, from the state's answer to them. Vertical keiretsu are manufacturer-centred pyramids of suppliers and distributors organized around a single core company and its product chain. The distinction is real but porous: Yonezawa Motors borrows from a horizontal group's bank, and Kizan Systems sells to two of them.

The "six major keiretsu" framing is itself a moving target. The post-war core was the four horizontal groups; Yonezawa Motors rose to comparable weight in the 1970s as the first major vertical; and Kizan Systems was added to the framing after 2008, when its state-seeded growth made the older vocabulary look incomplete. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry's Industry Bureau now "oversees the six" as a single portfolio of strategic sectors, and the Keiretsu Council admits their chairmen as a bloc — even though Kizan, which owns no bank shares and convenes no presidents' council, is a member of no traditional keiretsu at all. Abroad the word has a harder edge: in Baliscan cooperative discourse a "keiretsu" is shorthand for hierarchical capital as such. In Sanese usage it is descriptive, and within the business world, quietly proud.

==Origins==
{{Main|Zaibatsu}}

===The four great zaibatsu===
The keiretsu descend from the {{nihongo|Four Great Zaibatsu|四大財閥|Shidai Zaibatsu}} — Matsudaira, Aizawa, Mitoyo, and Shirakawa — which between them owned most of pre-war Sanese heavy industry, finance, and media. The {{nihongo|Matsudaira zaibatsu|松平財閥|Matsudaira Zaibatsu}}, the oldest and politically most central, began as a merchant bank in Okami in 1683, founded by a clan related to the ruling Honda dynasty; incorporated on 15 March 1872 in the creation season of the Saito Restoration, it used privileged access to government contracts and the shogunate's Sankoku trade networks to expand into steel, shipbuilding, and heavy machinery. The {{nihongo|Aizawa zaibatsu|相沢財閥|Aizawa Zaibatsu}} was a regional mercantile and chemical house whose financial arm, the {{nihongo|Seifu Bank|西府銀行|Seifu Ginkō}}, was founded in 1898 as the Aizawa prefectural industrial bank; its petrochemical interests were concentrated on Ishijima. The {{nihongo|Mitoyo zaibatsu|水豊財閥|Mitoyo Zaibatsu}} was the youngest, the exemplar of the {{nihongo|"new zaibatsu"|新興財閥|shinkō zaibatsu}} that rose outside the Restoration-era charmed circle: a railway-centred house chartered on 12 November 1912, which bought the land along its rights-of-way before announcing its routes and captured in real estate, department stores, and hotels the value its own trains created. The {{nihongo|Shirakawa zaibatsu|白川財閥|Shirakawa Zaibatsu}}, smallest and least militarized, held publishing, newspapers, and insurance, and cultivated the archipelago's emerging entertainment industry; under patriarch Masahiro Shirakawa it was an early investor in Hoshi Productions.

===War profiteering and the armistice settlement===
All four houses profiteered from the [[Great Adonian War]], into which Okaiken entered in 1947 under its Mitoyagi–Zong Treaty obligations, supplying the war economy at guaranteed margins. The Zong bombing campaign destroyed roughly 40% of Sanese industrial capacity; the zaibatsu's balance sheets, held in land and overseas instruments, did not share the damage. The [[Armistice of Okami]], signed on 28 April 1952 under Mosellan mediation, ended the war without occupation: the empire retained its sovereignty, its institutions, and its home islands. But the settlement's operative provisions were economic, and the first of them was aimed at the houses. The Illypnian coalition's war aims had included the elimination of the concentrations of economic power that had organized the war economy, and the settlement accordingly required the Sanese government to dissolve the family holding companies, disperse the families' controlling equity, and sever the interlocking directorates that bound the groups' member companies.

The implementing package of Prime Minister [[Munenori Mitsui]]'s government — the Progressives, who assumed office in the war's final months and signed the settlement on armistice day — was genuinely enforced at its edges: the holding companies were wound up, the family names came off the letterheads, the families' controlling stakes in the first-tier operating companies were sold, and family members were barred from executive office in the groups' financial arms. Its core was never carried through. The ministries charged with administering the dissolution were the same ministries that had spent eighty years partnering with the houses; the Bank of Okaiken and the Ministry of Finance blocked any dissolution of the Matsudaira bank itself, on the correct grounds that the empire's payment system ran through it; and the families restructured into trusts, foundations, and nominee shareholdings faster than the legislation could define them. The political turn came in 1956, when Ichirō Hatoyama's conservative Democrats took power on the {{nihongo|"Reverse Course"|逆コース|Gyaku Kōsu}} platform — rapid industrial reconstruction and suppression of the labour left, judged a precondition of the Echian investment the new government sought — halting further dissolution and channelling the new Okaiken Development Bank's directed credit through the very structures the settlement had ordered broken. The dissolution era closed with the system formally dissolved and functionally intact.

===Reorganization: 1952–1955===
What the dissolution era produced was not destruction but mutation. Between 1952 and the mid-1950s the houses reorganized themselves as keiretsu, and the reorganization was substantially complete by 1953. The family holding company was replaced by a lattice of cross-shareholdings among the member companies themselves, each holding modest stakes in the others; the patriarch was replaced by a monthly {{nihongo|presidents' council|社長会|shachōkai}}; and the family bank was reconstituted as the group's main bank. There was no longer any family apex to dissolve, because there was no longer any apex at all — only a circle of independent companies, each legally separate, each owning a little of the others, each coordinated by its bank and its council. Whether this constituted compliance with the settlement or evasion of it became the founding question of Sanese political economy, and it has never been answered to everyone's satisfaction.

Two institutions were new rather than reorganized. The first preceded the armistice: on 1 June 1948 the Ministry of Finance had encouraged the merger of the Okami Trust Bank (founded 1923) and Okami Life Insurance (founded 1931) into the [[Okami Financial Group]] ({{nihongo|大紙金融グループ|Ōkami Kin'yū Gurūpu}}), a deliberately assembled counterweight to the Matsudaira monopoly in deposit-taking. Under its founding chairman, the purged finance bureaucrat Shigeru Okami, OFG was built as a keiretsu from the ground up, with its own cross-shareholding network of insurance, real estate, and media companies and no affiliation with the older industrial clans. The second was the renaming of the oldest house: in 1953 the reorganized Matsudaira industrial holdings adopted the neutral name {{nihongo|Sangoku|三國|"three lands"}}, drawn from the archipelago's historic Sankoku designation, to shed the clan association the settlement had made a liability. The bank kept the Matsudaira name; the group's corporate lending arm, spun out as the {{nihongo|Sangoku Bank|三國銀行|Sangoku Ginkō}}, took the new one. The same year the four horizontal groups founded the Keiretsu Council. The Aizawa interests coalesced into the [[Aizawa Consortium]] around Seifu Bank, and the Mitoyo railway empire into [[Mitoyo Holdings]] around the {{nihongo|Mitoyo Trust Bank|水豊信託銀行|Mitoyo Shintaku Ginkō}} — the descendant of the group trust company the Mitoyo had pioneered before the war. The Shirakawa house alone declined full integration, and its successor, the [[Shirakawa Group]], remains an independent mid-tier group to the present day.

==Structure==
The keiretsu is less a corporate form than a set of interlocking practices. Four of them are structural — cross-shareholdings, the main bank system, the presidents' councils, and the supplier pyramid — and a fifth, coordination with the economic ministries, is political. None of the practices is unique to Okaiken; the combination is.

===Cross-shareholdings===
The signature mechanism is reciprocal equity. Member companies hold small stakes in one another — typically between one and five percent — and in the group's bank; the bank in turn holds blocks in each major member. Matsudaira Bank, for example, holds 4.2% of [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]], 3.8% of [[Yonezawa Motors]], and 3.5% of [[Soma Steelworks]]. No single stake confers control; the web as a whole does, by making exit visible, expensive, and disloyal.

The practice has three consequences that define the system. It stabilizes ownership against hostile acquisition, since friendly shareholders hold the float. It aligns horizons, since a member's shareholders are its customers, suppliers, and bank. And it insulates management from market discipline, since the shareholders best placed to replace a board are the least inclined to. Defenders call the first two consequences strengths and the third their price; critics call all three the same phenomenon by different names. The [[Banking Transparency Act]] of 2024, which caps a bank's equity stake in any single non-financial company at 5% and its aggregate equity holdings at 100% of Tier 1 capital, is the first statutory attack on the web as such.

===The main bank system===
{{Main|Main bank system}}
The {{nihongo|main bank system|メインバンク制|Mein Banku Sei}} designates one lender as a company's primary source of credit, its shareholder, its monitor in distress, and often its rescuer of last resort. The main bank holds the largest lending share and an equity block, seats directors on the client's board, coordinates rescue finance when the client falters, and expects in exchange the client's deposits, underwriting business, and loyalty. The system's strength was shock absorption: a company in temporary trouble could borrow against its relationship rather than its collateral, and the group could reallocate capital to whichever member needed it. Its weakness was what it became in the 1990s — evergreen lending, the rolling over of loans to insolvent borrowers so that neither the loan nor the relationship had to be marked down. The zombie enterprises of the Lost Decade were not accidents of the main bank system; they were the system operating under stress.

Each horizontal group is organized around its own main bank. The Sangoku Group borrows from the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]] ({{nihongo|松平金融グループ|Matsudaira Kin'yū Gurūpu}}), the largest financial institution in Okaiken with 円4.1 trillion in assets, within which [[Matsudaira Bank]] handles retail and commercial business and the Sangoku Bank corporate and industrial lending. The Aizawa Consortium borrows from [[Seifu Bank of Okaiken|Seifu Bank]]; Mitoyo Holdings from the Mitoyo Trust Bank; and the Okami Financial Group from itself, its banking arm performing the same function for its insurance and securities subsidiaries. The vertical groups borrow from a horizontal group's bank — Yonezawa Motors' main bank is Matsudaira Bank, which is why the automotive group sits inside the Sangoku orbit without belonging to it. Kizan Systems has no main bank at all: its anchor lender is the state-owned [[Okaiken Development Bank]], with commercial credit from Seifu and Toyozawa-Daiichi Bank.

===Presidents' councils===
Each horizontal group convenes a {{nihongo|presidents' council|社長会|shachōkai}} — a monthly meeting of member-company presidents, with no legal standing, where positions are aligned before anything is decided anywhere else. Their names are the groups' private shorthand: the {{nihongo|Sangoku-kai|三國会}} for Sangoku, the {{nihongo|Aizawa-kai|相沢会}} for Aizawa, the {{nihongo|Mitoyo-kai|水豊会}} for Mitoyo, and the {{nihongo|Ōkami-kai|大紙会}} for the Okami Financial Group. The vertical groups convene no councils of their own — Yonezawa's supplier network is coordinated from the top of the pyramid, and Kizan coordinates its ecosystem by contract rather than by custom. Positions agreed in the councils feed upward into the Keiretsu Council, where the groups speak with one voice to the state.

===Trading companies and the group at the centre===
Each horizontal group was organized around the same triad: a main bank that provided primary financing, a trading company that handled domestic and international commerce, and a constellation of manufacturing subsidiaries. The trading arms moved the group's steel, ships, chemicals, and railcars to market and financed the group's customers; after the bubble burst, the Shendan operations of the Sangoku and Aizawa groups — trade with Zong and Dagit — kept the shipyards and chemical plants working when domestic orders collapsed. The triad was the Miracle's delivery mechanism: the bank supplied patient capital, the trading arm supplied market access, and the manufacturing constellation supplied the capacity the state's vision documents called for.

===Supplier networks and the dual labour structure===
Vertical keiretsu organize production as a pyramid: a core manufacturer at the apex, first-tier suppliers bound by equity and long-term contract below it, and beneath them tiers of subcontractors bound only by dependence. Yonezawa Motors sits above [[Usami Heavy Industries]] and [[Nanyo Electronics]] and the tiers beneath them; Kizan Systems above the independent Gunma precision firms and the cross-keiretsu supplier [[Shoko Denko]]. The pyramid delivers quality and just-in-time discipline. It also delivers the dual labour structure for which the system is criticized: core workers — roughly a third of the industrial labour force at the Miracle's height — held lifetime employment, {{nihongo|nenkō|年功}} seniority wages, enterprise unions, and company housing, while the subcontractor tiers, holding none of these, absorbed the shocks the core was insulated from. By the 2020s non-regular employment had risen to 37% of the national workforce, and the boundary between core and periphery had become the labour market's defining inequality.

===Ministry coordination===
The keiretsu never operated alone; they operated inside Guided Capitalism. MITI (established 1949, renamed the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry in the 2001 reorganization) issued the vision documents that identified target industries, and the groups aligned their investment plans with them; the [[Okaiken Development Bank]], founded in 1953, lent on the state's designation where private banks would not — its 1956 loan to Soma Steelworks, extended against land collateral when no private bank would lend against rubble, is the canonical case. Coordination ran through personnel as well as paper: retired ministry officials {{nihongo|descend from heaven|天下り|amakudari}} into group directorships, and group executives ascend into ministry advisory councils. The practice has its critics' word for it — regulatory capture — and its defenders' word — public–private partnership. The November 2023 "[[Shadow Industrial Policy]]" exposé, in which the ''Okami Financial Chronicle'' documented the Industry Bureau convening informal {{nihongo|morning assemblies|朝礼|chōrei}} with Sangoku and Aizawa executives to pre-coordinate 円8.7 billion in subsidies and tax breaks between 2019 and 2023 — confirmed by the Board of Audit in 2024 — suggested which word fits the 2020s.

==The six major keiretsu==

The six major keiretsu are not six equal things. Four are horizontal groups — three descended from the dissolved zaibatsu and one assembled by the state as their counterweight; two are vertical manufacturing empires that grew into the framing as their weight grew. Together they account for roughly the industrial sector's 27.4% share of GDP, and all six are headquartered in Okami.

