===Sangoku Group===
{{Main|Sangoku Group}}
The {{nihongo|Sangoku Group|三國グループ|Sangoku Gurūpu}} is the largest keiretsu and the direct heir of the Matsudaira zaibatsu. It is built around heavy industry: [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] ({{nihongo|三國重工業|Sangoku Jūkōgyō}}), whose Aizawa shipyards are the world's largest exporter of LNG carriers and whose Toyozawa yard in the Konohana ward — 8,200 workers, eighteen large vessels a year — and Seto yard on the Katsuran Strait produce a large share of the national merchant marine; [[Soma Steelworks]], the ODB's first great client; and a construction division that grew through the Miracle into the largest in the country before being spun off in 1994. Its main bank is the [[Matsudaira Financial Group]], the largest financial institution in Okaiken, within which Matsudaira Bank holds the group's retail and commercial business and the Sangoku Bank its corporate and industrial lending.

The group's modern reach is wider than its steel. [[Sanesecom]], the incumbent telecommunications carrier, is 35%-owned by Sangoku, and its chairman, Hiroshi Yamamoto, sits on the Keiretsu Council in the group's colours. Sangoku Heavy Industries builds the {{nihongo|Tairyū|大竜}}-class deep-sea mining submersibles and the {{nihongo|Sangoku Float|三國フロート}} semi-submersible offshore wind platforms, and supplies deep-sea mining vessels to the [[Seabed Oversight and Maritime Agency]] established in 2018. After the bubble, Shendan trade contracts with Zong and Dagit kept the shipyards working when domestic orders collapsed; in June 2026 the group booked a 円12 billion container-vessel order from Baliscan Shipping, the first major Baliscan order placed with a Sanese heavy industrial group. In 2018 it introduced {{nihongo|Tesshin-kinmu|鉄身勤務|"Iron Body" work discipline}} — company-wide morning calisthenics and a dress code — as a productivity ritual; critics read it as culture-war theatre, and its adoption across the heavy industrial firms made it a symbol of the keiretsu workplace. The group's presidents' council is the {{nihongo|Sangoku-kai|三國会}}.

To capitalize on frontier ocean and geothermal reserves, the group established [[Sangoku Abyssal Dynamics]] and [[Sangoku Geothermal Extraction]] in 2024. Sangoku Abyssal Dynamics leads heavy subsea robotics and deep-sea power cabling operations along the Shendan Trench, engineering autonomous benthic extractors and pressure-resistant submersibles capable of operating below four thousand meters. Sangoku Geothermal Extraction operates high-pressure closed-loop brine refineries across the volcanic geothermal fields of [[Ishiwara Prefecture|Ishiwara]] and [[Shinagawa Prefecture|Shinagawa]], extracting battery-grade lithium carbonate and rare minerals directly from subterranean volcanic brines before reinjecting steam back into thermal aquifers.

===Aizawa Consortium===
{{Main|Aizawa Consortium}}
The {{nihongo|Aizawa Consortium|相沢コンソーシアム|Aizawa Konsōshiamu}} descends from the Aizawa zaibatsu and is organized around chemicals, pharmaceuticals, and — since the late 1980s — telecommunications. Its main bank is [[Seifu Bank of Okaiken|Seifu Bank]], founded in 1898 as the Aizawa prefectural industrial bank and now the second-largest private bank in Okaiken with 円3.2 trillion in assets and 45% of retail deposits in western Okaiken and the Yagikoshu region. The group's separately listed trust bank, [[Aizawa Trust]] ({{nihongo|相沢信託銀行|Aizawa Shintaku Ginkō}}), holds 円820 billion in assets — principally the pension funds of the member companies and the western public retirement systems.

The consortium's chemical division dominates the Tokuyama Plastics Corridor, which produces roughly 40% of the national output of engineering plastics, and its petrochemical complex on Ishijima supplies the feedstock. [[Aizawa Telecom]] ({{nihongo|相沢テレコム|Aizawa Terekomu}}), the group's flagship since it launched the country's first commercial mobile service in 1988, has about 52 million subscribers and revenue of 円248 billion in FY2025; it was the first carrier to deploy 5G, in December 2019, and it deploys [[Kizan Systems]] T-9 tensor processors in its base stations for edge computing — a transaction that crosses every boundary the old system drew, a horizontal group's telecom buying a new-style group's silicon. Its chief technology officer, Maya Aizawa, is of the founding family. [[Aizawa Cable]] lays the group's fibre; [[Aizawa Electronics]] makes its components; the pharmaceutical division rounds out a portfolio whose breadth is the horizontal keiretsu's signature. The group's council is the {{nihongo|Aizawa-kai|相沢会}}.

In response to evolving environmental standards and regional bio-resource mandates, the consortium founded [[Aizawa Advanced Polymers]] and [[Aizawa Subsea Communications]] in 2025. Aizawa Advanced Polymers bridges the chemical refining capacity of the Tokuyama Plastics Corridor with Harujima's coastal seafood processing networks, converting discarded crustacean and mackerel biomass into high-tensile chitosan-nanosilica biocomposites that achieve vibrant structural coloration without synthetic petrochemical dyes. Aizawa Subsea Communications constructs high-capacity, pressure-armored fiber optic trunk lines connecting the coastal data clusters of Harujima with the Yagikoshu Data Corridor and the outer island networks of Sanzu.

===Mitoyo Holdings===
{{Main|Mitoyo Holdings}}
[[Mitoyo Holdings]], legally the {{nihongo|Mitoyo Tetsudo Corporation|水豊鉄道株式会社|Mitoyo Tetsudō Kabushiki-gaisha}}, is the railway keiretsu: the descendant of the Mitoyo zaibatsu, organized around the [[Mitoyo Electric Railway]] ({{nihongo|水豊電気鉄道|Mitoyo Denki Tetsudō}}) and the property, retail, and hospitality empire the railway made possible. It is the clearest surviving example of the railway-conglomerate model: the company that runs the trains also owns the land the trains made valuable, the department stores at the termini, and the hotels above the stations. Its main bank is the {{nihongo|Mitoyo Trust Bank|水豊信託銀行|Mitoyo Shintaku Ginkō}}; its council is the {{nihongo|Mitoyo-kai|水豊会}}.

The group extended its transport dominance from surface rail into pneumatic and subsea logistics through [[Mitoyo Pneumatic Transit]] and [[Mitoyo Marine Logistics]], chartered in 2024. Anchored by heavy engineering yards in [[Toyozawa Metropolis|Toyozawa]] and [[Seto Prefecture|Seto]], Mitoyo Pneumatic Transit harnesses the extreme fourteen-meter tidal heads of Toyozawa Bay to charge subsea pneumatic accumulator caverns. These high-pressure chambers propel sealed variable-buoyancy freight pods through submerged tubes spanning the Katsuran Strait, moving intermodal freight between Aizawa Port and Toyozawa entirely independent of surface typhoons and maritime shipping lanes.

The Mitoyo Electric Railway itself, founded on 12 November 1912, operates 164.2 kilometres of standard-gauge line and 88 stations across northwestern Yagikoshu, carries about 1.12 million passengers a day, and earned 円342.8 billion in FY2025 with 7,850 consolidated employees. It opened its flagship Ichijoudani Line in 1916 along the southern banks of the Yodo and Shizuku rivers, ran the country's first bi-level double-decker commuter carriages in 1978, and introduced its reserved-seating "Premium Car" service in 2017; its chief rival is the patrician [[Toyokyu]], which built along the affluent northern hills while Mitoyo routed through the wholesale districts and riverfront markets. The holding company is headquartered in Okami with the other five groups, but the group's operational centre of gravity remains [[Toyozawa Metropolis]], where the railway is headquartered at Minami-Toyo Station.

===Okami Financial Group===
{{Main|Okami Financial Group}}
The {{nihongo|Okami Financial Group|大紙金融グループ|Ōkami Kin'yū Gurūpu}} is the system's pure financial keiretsu and its only founding member that was never a zaibatsu. Assembled by the Ministry of Finance on 1 June 1948 from the Okami Trust Bank (1923) and Okami Life Insurance (1931) as a counterweight to the Matsudaira monopoly, it is the fourth-largest financial institution in Okaiken, with 円1.4 trillion in assets and 31,500 employees. Its four subsidiaries — Okami Bank (円980 billion in deposits across 412 branches), Okami Life, the country's second-largest life insurer, Okami Securities, and Okami Trust — make it one of the three banking pillars, with Sangoku Bank and Aizawa Trust, that together hold roughly 45% of domestic deposits. Its council is the {{nihongo|Ōkami-kai|大紙会}}.

OFG's history is the financial system's history in miniature. It expanded aggressively through the bubble — its real estate lending grew from 円80 billion in 1980 to 円420 billion in 1989, and its capital conduit to the Nakamizuan offshore financial centre became a byword for opacity — and it took a 円22 billion public injection in 1994. But because it recognized its losses earlier than its rivals, it emerged from the Lost Decade with the strongest balance sheet of the three pillars, and under chairman Yusaku Tendo (since 2014) it has grown assets under management from 円580 billion in 2015 to 円1.4 trillion in 2025, offsetting the margin compression of the negative interest rate era. The Banking Transparency Act has limited direct impact on the group, which holds fewer cross-shareholdings than the industrial groups; the En Crisis of January–February 2026 cost it about 円8 billion in mark-to-market losses on government bonds; and during the Satori AI Blackout its insurance division's manual-processing capacity — a legacy of paper-era compliance — became a national asset. The group's patronage of professional sport, including League One rugby's Okami Sungoliath, is a brand-building instrument unusual among Sanese financial groups.

===Yonezawa Motors===
{{Main|Yonezawa Motors}}
[[Yonezawa Motors]] ({{nihongo|米沢自動車|Yonezawa Jidōsha}}) is the automotive keiretsu and the first vertical group admitted to the major framing. Founded in 1971 by Aoyama Kenshin (born 1948), it grew through the Sanese Recovery of the 1970s into the core of a supplier pyramid that anchors the Yonezawa Industrial Zone, part of the Greater Okami industrial belt established in 1954. Its flagship plant in the Zone employs 18,000 workers and produces more than 400,000 vehicles a year, with additional capacity at Harujima; the national industry it leads produces 8.5 million vehicles a year, the fourth-largest output in the world. Its main bank is Matsudaira Bank, which holds 3.8% of its equity — a horizontal group's bank anchoring a vertical group's growth, the clearest case of the two structures interlocking.

Yonezawa's tier-one suppliers — [[Usami Heavy Industries]] for rolling stock and construction equipment, [[Nanyo Electronics]] for electronics and precision components — and the subcontractor tiers beneath them reproduce the vertical keiretsu in its classic form. The group rode the bubble on luxury sedans, survived its aftermath on exports, and in the 2020s is executing the industry's hydrogen and solid-state battery transition, with the Yonezawa Motorsports Complex as its public face. Its independence from the horizontal groups is relative rather than absolute: it buys from them, sells to them, and borrows from them, but no presidents' council convenes in its name.

In the mid-2020s, Yonezawa accelerated its technological divergence from traditional internal combustion through two specialized engineering subsidiaries, [[Yonezawa Solid-State Technologies]] and [[Yonezawa Hydro-Mobility]]. Capitalizing on high-purity lithium carbonate and mineral feedstocks sourced from the northern geothermal extraction belts, Yonezawa Solid-State Technologies manufactures next-generation ceramic-electrolyte automotive battery cells designed for rapid sub-zero charging. Concurrently, Yonezawa Hydro-Mobility develops heavy fuel-cell powertrains and pneumatic kinetic booster systems for commercial lorries, municipal transit fleets, and specialized freight locomotives.

===Kizan Systems===
{{Main|Kizan Systems}}
[[Kizan Systems]] ({{nihongo|木山システムズ株式会社|Kizan Shisutemuzu Kabushiki-gaisha}}) is the semiconductor and artificial-intelligence group and the system's designated future. It began in 1991 as the Kizan Semiconductor Research Laboratory — twelve researchers in Yonezawa Prefecture under Kizan Toshiaki (born 1968), a former [[Nakajima Electronics]] engineer — and spent fifteen years in obscurity on contract work for automakers and small defence projects. Incorporated on 15 June 2008 under the Digital Infrastructure Act, with 円2.3 billion in state equity and grants, it grew revenue from 円180 billion in 2010 to 円890 billion in 2016 and 円1.87 trillion in 2024, and now employs 48,200 people. It is the second-largest technology company in Okaiken by market capitalization, behind the independent Nakajima Electronics, and its T-7 and T-9 tensor processors are the hardware backbone of Sanese artificial intelligence — from the [[Satori AI]] platform, whose Sky-Sync rail-scheduling systems have cut fuel consumption by 12% on the lines they manage, to Aizawa Telecom's base stations.

Kizan is a keiretsu in the vertical tradition and a member of no traditional keiretsu. It has no main bank — its anchor lender is the state-owned Okaiken Development Bank — no presidents' council, and no cross-shareholding web; its suppliers (the independent Gunma Precision Works, Iijima Robotics, and Katsuran Semiconductor Equipment, and the cross-keiretsu [[Shoko Denko]]) are bound by contract rather than equity. Its incorporation was the deliberate centrepiece of the Digital Infrastructure Act's break with keiretsu collateral lending: the state seeded it with equity and grants, not loans against land. Whether the result escapes the old system is the open question. Kizan's 円890 million in no-bid defence contracts (2018–2022), its 円500 million in donations to the Liberal Democratic Party (2015–2020), the Fair Trade Commission's 円12 billion settlement over supplier pressure in 2024, the amakudari directors on its board, and the lawsuit filed by the founder's brother Masahiro in 2025 have earned the group its epitaph in the financial press: "too integral to fail, too compromised to trust."

Beyond terrestrial data centers, Kizan has pioneered oceanic compute infrastructure through its specialized offshoot, [[Kizan Abyssal Computing]], founded in late 2024. Capitalizing on the steep bathymetry of the Shendan Trench, Kizan Abyssal Computing deploys modular, titanium-jacketed data pods into subsea abyssal waters at depths exceeding one thousand meters. By circulating two-degree oceanic waters through passive heat exchangers, these subsea clusters cool massive tensor processing arrays without evaporative fresh water or active chillers, achieving a power usage effectiveness of 1.04 while slashing cooling overhead by over eight hundred megawatts.

===Independents and the boundary of the system===
Not everything large in Sanese business is a keiretsu, and the boundary has always been porous. [[Shoko Denko]] (OSE: 6450), founded on 1 April 1970 under an Okaiken Development Bank credit programme with Aizawa Consortium co-capital, supplies Kizan Systems and Sangoku Heavy Industries alike and is the model of the cross-keiretsu supplier that survives by serving every group and belonging to none. [[Nakajima Electronics]], the largest technology company in the country by market capitalization, has remained independent since its founding. [[Hotta Holdings]], the post-war construction and real estate dynasty, cultivates "traditional zaibatsu independence in style only". [[Kawashima Motors]] competes with Yonezawa as an independent family firm; the [[Shirakawa Group]] carries the fourth zaibatsu's name into the mid-tier as an independent media and insurance house; [[Minatojima Shipyard]], founded in 1905 by Hoshino Seiji without zaibatsu capital, remains independent; and the Gunma machine-tool firms of the interior highlands supply all the groups while belonging to none. The list matters for what it shows: since 2008 the state has deliberately widened the space outside the system, and the independents have filled it.

===Mid-tier champions and specialized regional corridors===
Beyond the six major keiretsu conglomerates, the Sanese industrial ecosystem is profoundly shaped by independent medium-sized manufacturing champions, known as {{nihongo|chūken kigyō|中堅企業}}. These specialized, highly competitive enterprises dominate critical market niches across the four island divisions, often supplying patented components, high-purity chemical precursors, and advanced machinery to both domestic keiretsu networks and overseas partners.

In the northern geothermal corridors of [[Ishiwara Prefecture|Ishiwara]], [[Ishiwara Brine Technologies]] operates commercial ion-exchange separation arrays that filter rare alkali metals, boron, and battery-grade lithium directly from supercritical volcanic discharge. In Harujima, [[Tokuyama Marine Biomaterials]] leads regional chitin purification, transforming municipal shellfish waste into biodegradable medical sutures, food-grade bioplastics, and structural-color textiles. Biopharmaceutical synthesis is led by [[Shinagawa Marine Biopharma]] in [[Shinagawa Prefecture|Shinagawa]], which cultures barophilic extremophile micro-organisms harvested from hydrothermal abyssal vents to synthesize neuro-regenerative peptides, squalene, and adaptogenic compounds, working closely with the athletic nutrition industry of the [[Cult of Iron]] and Vaiahi botanical researchers.

Industrial automation and specialized logistics are supported by specialized engineering champions centered around the Seto Inland Sea and the Sanzu division. [[Suzuka Kinetic Engineering]] in [[Suzuka Prefecture|Suzuka]] manufactures heavy pneumatic accumulators, high-pressure caissons, and precision fluid-power actuators used in tidal power stations and automated port cranes. In [[Seto Prefecture|Seto]], the [[Katsuran Subsea Transit Corporation]] operates the commercial pneumatic tube rail line beneath the Katsuran Strait, shuttling high-value electronics and refrigerated seafood between the ports of Harujima and Sanzu. Sanzu's regional economic integration is further reinforced through its administrative and transport corridor: [[Tosa Prefecture|Tosa]] anchors the southern agricultural and maritime logistics of the Inland Sea, while the archipelagic prefecture of [[Shiogami Prefecture|Shiogami]] serves as the nominal maritime anchor of Sanzu, hosting independent marine salvage, oceanic telemetry, and inter-island ferry cooperatives that interface directly with the national shipping lines.

==History==

===The Industrial Miracle (1955–1975)===
{{Main|Sanese Industrial Miracle}}
The keiretsu were built for reconstruction and delivered it. The First Reconstruction Boom (1955–1958) restarted the industrial core; the Honda Boom (1961–1964), driven by Prime Minister Haruki Honda's Income Doubling Plan, doubled wages and with them the consumer market the keiretsu served; and the Shinkansen Boom (1966–1970) wired the country together with infrastructure the keiretsu built. Crude steel output rose from 6.2 million tons in 1955 to 79 million tons in 1975; the Aizawa shipyards became the world's largest exporter of LNG carriers; and the Yonezawa Industrial Zone grew into the automotive heartland. Real wages tripled, nine million people migrated to the cities, and the "three treasures" of lifetime employment, seniority wages, and enterprise unions were extended — unevenly — across the core.

The system's contribution was coordination. The Okaiken Development Bank lent where private banks would not: its 1956 loan to Soma Steelworks was extended against land collateral on MITI's designation, when no private bank would lend against rubble, and the land's twelvefold appreciation over the following decade became the quiet arithmetic on which the collateral-lending model was built. The main banks followed the ODB's lead; the councils aligned investment across supply chains; cross-subsidization absorbed shocks inside the groups before they reached the labour market. MITI's vision documents identified the targets and the keiretsu supplied the capital, the managers, and the patience. The Miracle was not a market outcome, and it was not a state outcome; it was the joint product of a state that could not invest and groups that would.

The costs were real and unevenly distributed. The core workforce received the full keiretsu bargain; the subcontractor tiers received the orders, the deadlines, and the risk. Industrial smog and the pollution litigation of the era exposed the environmental bill, and the migration emptied the countryside. But in 1975, when the Miracle ended, the keiretsu were the most admired institutions in Okaiken, and the model was being studied — and feared — across Adonia.

===Black Monday and the Quiet Revolution (1967–1970)===
The first test of the system was political. The Darasian–Zong Trade War severed the sea lanes on which Sanese exports depended, and on 15 October 1967 the Okami Stock Exchange crashed — the Black Monday that began the long crisis of the post-war order. The Ohno austerity programme (1970–1973) and the Quiet Revolution of 1968–1970 followed: the Sanese Empire was dissolved on 15 April 1970, the Federal Republic was proclaimed, and the country took the name Okaiken on 12 July 1971. The state was rebuilt from the foundations; the keiretsu were not. The groups held employment and investment through the turbulence — a performance that deepened their social legitimacy and, in the view of later critics, their conviction that they were too central to be allowed to fail.

===The Sanese Recovery (1973–1976)===
{{Main|Sanese Recovery}}
The Recovery government of Prime Minister Yasuhiro Nakasone restored growth through deregulation, reconciliation, and public works: the Shinkansen reached Aizawa and Toyozawa, the construction sector expanded into the "Concrete State", and exports resumed. It was in these years that Yonezawa Motors rose to national weight, and the automotive vertical joined the four horizontal groups in what was now called "the major keiretsu" — the first change in the framing since 1953.

===The 1976–1981 crisis===
The second test was external, and the system failed it. The Camboriú War of November 1976 and the Bight of Ayorin Crisis closed export markets; the Marañón oil fires destroyed 612 wells, about 9% of Adonian Common market energy production, and the energy price shock hit a country 78% dependent on imported oil. The International Denar's peg to the Adonian Mark imported the Mark Area's deflation wholesale. Stagflation followed; and when the peg was finally suspended on 9 October 1979, the en was devalued 23% and the OSE 225 fell 38%. Through it all the keiretsu maintained production and employment as demand collapsed, cross-subsidizing losses out of group reserves. The performance preserved social stability in the short run and accumulated structural excess capacity that would haunt the economy for decades. The 1976–1981 crisis is the first documented case of what would later be called keiretsu rigidity: the system's shock absorbers had become its suspension of reality.

===The Go-go 8090 (1980–1990)===
{{Main|Go-go 8090}}
The bubble began as a recovery. Prime Minister Okazaki's structural reforms — expressway privatization, the abolition of tariffs, the opening of the capital account — and the Financial Modernization Act of 1981, which let banks underwrite securities and lend against real estate collateral, released the Recovery years' pent-up liquidity into an asset market with nowhere else to go. Land prices in Okami rose 60% in a single year at the peak; the OSE 225 reached 39,000 shortly before New Year 1990; golf club memberships traded at 円50 million.

The keiretsu mutated with the market. The main banks — now organized as the three banking pillars of the Okami Financial Group, Sangoku Bank, and Aizawa Trust, holding roughly 45% of domestic deposits — lent against bubble land values, and the groups' investment coordination shifted from manufacturing capacity to real estate, construction, and finance. Matsudaira's real estate lending grew from 円120 billion in 1980 to 円680 billion in 1989; OFG's from 円80 billion to 円420 billion. Enzan Securities (founded 1984) and the Toyozawa Trust Bank — which lent against golf club memberships and became the first zombie bank — were the era's pure expressions; Sangoku Heavy Industries' construction division became the largest in the country; the Shintori and Dotonbori skylines were built with keiretsu credit. Even MITI became a participant rather than a guide: the credit direction of the late 1980s was shaped as much by keiretsu preferences as by ministry vision documents. The era's outward face was confident expansion — assembly plants and distribution centres in Balboa around Sant Cristòfor and the canal corridor, Shendan operations in Dagit, the first commercial mobile service from Aizawa Telecom in 1988 — and its inward face was the quiet substitution of land for production as the system's measure of worth.

===The Lost Decade and the Restructuring Era (1993–2000s)===
{{Main|Lost Decade (Okaiken)}}
When the bubble burst, the cross-shareholdings held and the truth did not. Real estate collateral became worthless faster than it could be marked: Matsudaira's non-performing loan ratio rose from 2% in 1989 to 12% in 1993; OFG's from 1.8% to 10%. Enzan Securities was dissolved in 1993, the Okami Land Development Corporation in 1992, the Shintori Investment Group in 1993. The response of the main bank system under stress was evergreen lending: rolling over loans to insolvent borrowers so that neither the loan nor the relationship needed to be recognized as impaired. The zombie banks kept the zombie enterprises alive; the zombie enterprises kept the zombie banks' balance sheets unimpaired; the cross-shareholdings made every participant someone else's shareholder; and the deflationary spiral did the rest. Matsudaira's political protection inside the ruling Liberal Democratic Party delayed the reckoning for the rest of the decade, through the Kaieda years (1995–1998) and their public-works anaesthesia.

The scandals that punctuated the decade were variations on one theme: the difference between what the groups said they were worth and what they were. The Suzuki Accounting Scandal of 1993 — inflated asset holdings across the keiretsu, in some cases by up to 340% — produced the Wealth Disclosure Act of 1996, which required firms with more than 円50 billion in assets to publish audited statements. The Golf Club Diplomacy scandal of March 1993, broken by a Baliscan newspaper from leaked internal records of Aizawa Trust's Shendan division, exposed golf club memberships as bribery instruments; the Okami Financial Group was named in the coverage, denied institutional involvement, and accepted the resignation of two executives. The Minamishinano earthquake of 1995 strained the construction groups to the point of national mobilization.

The reckoning, when it came, came from the state. Prime Minister Okada's zombie disposal programme (1998–2001) forced loss recognition and recapitalization: Matsudaira posted a net loss of 円85 billion in 1999 — the first loss in the 327-year history of the bank — took a 円40 billion public injection, and accepted the resignation of its chairman, Hideki Matsudaira. OFG, which had recognized earlier, reached a 4.5% NPL ratio by 1998 and emerged the strongest of the three pillars. The Restructuring Era ({{nihongo|再構築時代|Saikōchiku Jidai}}) — the name later given to the 1990s and 2000s as a whole — partially dismantled the system: Sangoku Heavy Industries spun off its construction division in 1994, Soma Steelworks' structural steel output fell 40%, lifetime employment eroded until non-regular employment reached 37% of the workforce, and the first voluntary cross-shareholding reductions began. The keiretsu survived the Lost Decade the way they had survived the armistice: diminished, restructured, and still at the centre of everything.

===The digital pivot (2001–2008)===
The 2000s began with the state learning to print money and ended with it learning to seed companies. The Bank of Okaiken Act amendments of 1998 gave the central bank independence, and quantitative easing began in 2001 — the start of the low-rate environment in which the keiretsu banks rebuilt their balance sheets and the asset-management economy took shape. In 2006 Prime Minister Hirota established the Truth and Reconciliation Commission — the "Radical Atonement" — to investigate the zaibatsu's complicity in the Great Adonian War; the groups' origins became, for the first time, a matter of public record rather than inherited lore.

The Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008 was the deliberate break. Its 円890 billion Yagikoshu Data Corridor, its ecosystem model of shared infrastructure for startups and multinationals, and above all its financing method — equity and grants rather than loans against collateral — were designed to break the keiretsu collateral-lending link and to seed companies that owed the state, not a main bank, their existence. Kizan Systems, incorporated on 15 June 2008, was the policy's proof of concept; the Miracle's lesson — that state-directed industrial policy works when the priorities are correct and the structures are temporary — was written into the Act's design. Whether the structures have been temporary is what the 2020s are testing.

===The Aoyagi assault (2016–2021)===
{{Main|Red Reckoning Reforms}}
The election of Sho Aoyagi's [[Sanese Communist Party]] government in 2016 was the first time the keiretsu faced a state that did not want them. Aoyagi's government framed the groups as {{nihongo|parasitic monopolies|寄生虫的独占|Kiseichū-teki Dokusen}} — concentrations of capital that extracted value without creating it — and its Red Reckoning Reforms legislated the framing: the statutory corporate tax rate rose from 23.2% to 28%, the effective rate paid by the largest groups from about 24% to 29.5%, and the Technology Investment Tax Credit was eliminated outright for firms with more than 円100 billion in revenue, a threshold that hit the six and almost no one else. A Wealth Solidarity Surcharge taxed concentrated fortunes; the negative interest rate policy introduced the same year compressed bank margins from 0.85% toward the 0.52% they would reach in the mid-2020s.

The assault, however, preserved what it taxed. Aoyagi's government never proposed dismantling the groups — the paradox critics on both wings noted — and the groups adapted through the fee-based economy: Matsudaira's assets under management grew from 円1.2 trillion to 円1.8 trillion across the Aoyagi years, OFG's from 円580 billion to 円1.4 trillion. When Prime Minister Honda Shigetsuma's government restored the statutory rate to 23.2% for FY2023 and reinstated the tax credit at 30%, the fiscal pendulum swung back; the rhetorical legacy did not. "Parasitic monopolies" entered the permanent political vocabulary, and every subsequent reform — the Banking Transparency Act included — has been argued in its shadow. In 2018 the Seabed Oversight and Maritime Agency was established with Sangoku-built deep-sea mining vessels, and the same year Sangoku introduced its Tesshin-kinmu morning calisthenics: the state and the groups had resumed their partnership without ever having dissolved it.

===The Matsudaira scandal and the Banking Transparency Act (2020–2024)===
{{Main|Matsudaira Financial Group|Banking Transparency Act}}
The scandal that finally broke the system's political protection unfolded in two phases. In the first (2020–2022), the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]] found that Matsudaira had concealed about 円120 billion in non-performing loans to Yagikoshu construction companies; the bank paid a 円45 billion fine, suspended its dividend for two years, and saw three executives imprisoned for between eighteen months and four years. Chairman Kenji Matsudaira took a 50% pay cut and a public apology, and resisted the calls for his resignation. In the second phase, a routine FSA inspection in September 2023 found a network of 23 special-purpose vehicles — the "Aizawa Ghost Fleet" of the financial press, for the Aizawa-registered shells that held the assets — concealing 円180 billion in bad loans in total and moving the bank's reported non-performing ratio from a printed 2.1% to an actual 6.8%. The vehicles had operated since about 2015, lending circularly among themselves and to LDP-connected construction companies and developers. The Sanese Communist Party demanded a criminal investigation; the Progressives called for nationalization; Kenji Matsudaira resigned, and a restructuring committee took control of compliance.

The Banking Transparency Act of 2024 ({{nihongo|銀行透明性法|Ginkō Tōmeisei Hō}}) was the legislative consequence. Its core provisions — monthly disclosure of loan books, consolidation of special-purpose vehicles, whistleblower rewards of up to 円50 million, independent audits for banks above 円500 billion in assets, and information exchange under the Adonian Common Reporting Standard — target concealment. Its cross-ownership provisions target the system: no bank may hold more than 5% of the equity of any single non-financial company, and aggregate equity holdings are capped at 100% of Tier 1 capital, with a five-year phase-in running from 2024 to 2029. The Keiretsu Council negotiated the phase-in as its most consequential recent campaign; the destination stands. The unwind is the first statutory dismantling of the cross-shareholding web since its construction, and its progress — steady, contested, and incomplete — is the system's vital statistic.

The scandal also broke the system's cover in a broader sense. The November 2023 "Shadow Industrial Policy" exposé — the Industry Bureau's informal morning assemblies with Sangoku and Aizawa executives, through which 円8.7 billion in subsidies and tax breaks was steered between 2019 and 2023, confirmed by the Board of Audit in 2024 — turned coordination from open secret to political scandal. In 2024 the FSA opened an investigation into the Hotta–Matsudaira cross-shareholding network under the Antimonopoly Act and the Wealth Disclosure Act, after a whistleblower disclosed that the network's holdings in the Nakamizuan offshore centre had been understated in disclosure filings. The Competitive Bidding Reform Act of 2024 closed the no-bid procurement channel through which Kizan Systems had taken 円890 million in defence contracts. And the Okaiken Wealth Organization's 2025 inheritance tax assessment — 円230 billion across the great family fortunes, including 円58 billion for the Hotta family and 円42 billion for the Matsudaira — reached into the private wealth the system had been designed to protect. The keiretsu had survived the armistice, the Quiet Revolution, the bubble, and the Communists; whether they survive their own bookkeeping is what the phase-in years will decide.

==The Keiretsu Council==
{{Main|Keiretsu Council}}
The {{nihongo|Keiretsu Council|経団連|Keidanren}}, formally the {{nihongo|経済団体連合会|Keizai Dantai Rengōkai}}, is the system's collective voice. Founded in Okami in 1953 by the four horizontal groups then completing their reorganization, it aggregates the positions of its member groups, presents a unified industrial front to the government, and organizes the private side of the quarterly [[Industrial Competitiveness Council]] ({{nihongo|産業競争力会議|Sangyō Kyōsoryoku Kaigi}}) through which the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry aligns state policy with corporate investment plans. A chairman drawn from the senior chairmanship of the member groups presides; the vice-chairmanship has been held, by unbroken convention since the founding, by the chairman of the Matsudaira Financial Group — at the founding by Yoshiaki Matsudaira, who led the bank from 1948 to 1972. Membership has expanded twice, from the four founding groups to include Yonezawa Motors in the 1970s and Kizan Systems after 2008. Together with the ministries and the Bank of Okaiken, the Council forms the private pillar of the iron triangle.

The Council is distinct from the other parts of the business establishment. The [[Okaiken Federation of Employers' Associations]], founded in Toyozawa on 7 September 1953 in the same season of institution-building, represents the employer side of the labour-management divide and holds formal status under the Association System Act; the Council is a private federation with no constitutional status, and its business is industrial policy, finance, taxation, and trade. Its founding purposes have proved durable: to stabilize the cross-shareholding arrangements, to present a single interface to the ministries, to ration investment between the groups so that reconstruction capital would not be wasted in intra-group competition, to bargain collectively over directed credit, tariffs, and subsidies, and to defend the successor system against any renewal of dissolution pressure. On the last count its record is mixed. It negotiated the Banking Transparency Act's phase-in, but the 2023 morning-assembly exposé forced both the ministry and the Council into the most significant transparency reforms in their history, and in 2026 the Council split openly over the STEP REP employment-mobility framework — its heavy-industry and construction members favouring employer flexibility, its Mercian-education and small-manufacturing members opposing. The split followed the group map of the economy rather than any ideological line, and it was the first such public division in decades.

==Criticism and debate==

===Insulation from market signals===
The system's core criticism is that it works too well at the wrong things. Cross-shareholdings stabilize ownership and horizons by removing the market's corrective function: a firm whose shareholders are its suppliers, customers, and bank cannot be disciplined by an exit, because its shareholders will not exit. The 1976–1981 crisis was the first demonstration — production maintained into collapsing demand — and the Lost Decade was the second, at a scale that cost the country a decade of growth. The Miracle's defenders reply with the counterfactual: the same insulation that prevented correction in 1979 prevented liquidation in 1953, and the country that needed patient capital in 1955 was the same country that could not afford it in 1995. The debate is unresolved because the evidence is the same evidence.

===Zombie lending and opacity===
Evergreen lending — rolling over the loans of insolvent borrowers to avoid recognizing losses — was the main bank system operating under stress, not a deviation from it. The zombie banks and zombie enterprises of the Lost Decade trapped capital in unproductive firms, deepened deflation, and delayed the reallocation recovery required. The concealment reflex outlived the practice: the Matsudaira special-purpose vehicles operated from about 2015 to 2023, through the very years in which the bank was publicly atoning for the 2020–2022 findings. The Banking Transparency Act's whistleblower rewards and monthly disclosure provisions are designed for an adversary that has proven inventive.

===Collusion and exclusion===
The coordination that aligned investment across supply chains also aligned prices and bids. The Fair Trade Commission's 2024 settlement with Kizan Systems — 円12 billion over supplier pressure — and the Competitive Bidding Reform Act's closure of the no-bid channel are the recent cases; the pre-coordinated subsidies documented in 2023 are the general form. The system has also been criticized for excluding outside suppliers: vertical keiretsu pyramids historically preferred group members, and foreign suppliers in particular found Sanese supply chains hard to enter. Baliscan cooperative discourse makes the same criticism structurally — a keiretsu is, in that vocabulary, hierarchy itself.

===The dual labour structure===
The keiretsu bargain — lifetime employment, seniority wages, enterprise unions, corporate welfare — was extended to roughly a third of the industrial workforce at its height, and to almost no one outside the core. The subcontractor tiers absorbed the shocks the core was insulated from: in downturns the pyramid's base bore the layoffs, the wage cuts, and the bankruptcies while the core's headcount held. By the 2020s non-regular employment had reached 37% of the national workforce, and the gap between core and periphery had become the labour market's defining inequality. Women have been historically underrepresented in the core's career tracks, and the enterprise unions bargain for members rather than for sectors — a structure that reproduces the boundary it defends.

===Regulatory capture===
The movement of officials between ministries and group directorships — {{nihongo|amakudari|天下り|amakudari|"descent from heaven"}} — has been documented since the Miracle, criticized since the Corporate Winter scandals of the mid-2000s, and legislated against since never. Kizan Systems' board includes former senior officials; the morning assemblies of 2019–2023 were convened by the Industry Bureau itself. The Bureau still "oversees the six" as a portfolio, and the quarterly Industrial Competitiveness Council is the institutionalized form of the practice. Critics call it a crisis of regulatory capture at the heart of the economic state; participants call it the machinery of Guided Capitalism working as designed.

===The defence===
The system's defenders make four arguments. Patient capital built the industrial economy: the Miracle's coordination could not have been assembled by markets alone, and no state agency could have invested it. Shock absorption preserved social stability through four systemic crises — the armistice settlement, Black Monday and the Quiet Revolution, the 1976–1981 collapse, and the Lost Decade — without the political ruptures that comparable economies suffered. Long horizons made the infrastructure possible: the Mitoyo railway, the Sangoku shipyards, and the Yonezawa plants were built on payback schedules no quarterly-reviewed firm would accept. And the alternative is not independence but another form of dependence: Kizan Systems, the designated future, owes its existence to the state's equity and its safety to the state's procurement — a keiretsu of one, with the Ministry as its main bank. The defence does not dispute the record; it disputes that any other record would have been better.

==Foreign operations==

===Balboa===
The groups' first major foreign presence was in Balboa, where 1980s foreign direct investment built assembly plants and distribution centres around Sant Cristòfor and the canal corridor. The investment was modest by later standards — Balboa's free trade zone, established in 1978, offered the assembly foothold, and the canal carries 8–10% of Sanese trade — but it established the pattern the groups still follow abroad: infrastructure-adjacent manufacturing, financed by a group bank, coordinated with the Ministry's export priorities.

===Balisca===
Balisca is the system's standing ideological antagonist and a growing commercial one. The Baliscan cooperative economy requires foreign investors to comply with worker self-management and community-benefit structures — a compliance burden that has limited keiretsu FDI to a fraction of what flows to Balboa — and the governing Avante! party has targeted Sanese corporate subsidiaries, Satori peripheral infrastructure, and the commercial networks of the Yagikoshu Data Corridor as vectors of "algorithmic capitalism". The 2.6 million Sanese Baliscans, the Instituto Baliscano de Economía Cooperativa, and a Mutual Cooperation Council give the relationship institutional depth beneath the rhetoric. The constitutional antagonism is real — in the Baliscan framing a keiretsu is hierarchy itself; in the Sanese framing the question is whether the forge produces good iron — but so is the commerce: in June 2026 the Sangoku Group booked a 円12 billion container-vessel order from Baliscan Shipping, the first major order placed by a Baliscan enterprise with a Sanese heavy industrial group.

===Varkana===
Varkana's communalist economy closes most strategic sectors to foreign capital, but its liberal approach to non-vital sectors has admitted Sanese distribution and light manufacturing. The flagship is the Sanko-Varkana joint venture — the Sangoku Group paired with a Varkan worker cooperative for electric-vehicle distribution — the closest thing the system has to an institutional experiment in cooperative compatibility; the Bank of Varkana maintains an Okami office that clears the trade. The pattern is Balisca's at smaller scale: ideological friction, commercial persistence.

===Kizan and the global technology market===
The newest foreign frontier is the semiconductor itself. Kizan Systems' T-7 and T-9 tensor processors are the primary domestic alternative to foreign AI hardware, and the group is Okaiken's entry in the global GPU competition; its dependence on extreme-ultraviolet lithography equipment from Breisland's VoxelTech is the strategic vulnerability that the Digital Sovereignty Act of June 2026 and the Technocratic Shield doctrine are built to close. The 2023 export restrictions threatened an eighteen-month delay to the 3-nanometre programme; the National Semiconductor Strategy that followed commits public capital to domestic lithography. The keiretsu went abroad first to buy resources, then to sell manufactures; the current generation is going abroad to secure the tools of its own replacement.

==Decline or transformation?==

The Banking Transparency Act's phase-in (2024–2029) is unwinding the cross-shareholding web at its core: the banks must bring aggregate equity holdings within 100% of Tier 1 capital and no single industrial stake above 5%, which requires the sale of most of the blocks Matsudaira Bank, Sangoku Bank, Aizawa Trust, and their peers have held since the 1950s. The unwind touches the ownership structure; it does not touch the rest of the system. The main bank relationships, the presidents' councils, the supplier pyramids, the trading operations, and the Keiretsu Council are untouched by the Act, and the groups have spent the phase-in's first years demonstrating that the network survives the ownership: Sangoku booked the Baliscan order in June 2026 with a balance sheet already in compliance.

What is changing is the system's composition and its centre of gravity. Kizan Systems — no main bank, no council of its own, no cross-shareholdings — is the fastest-growing of the six and the state's preferred instrument in semiconductors, artificial intelligence, and defence; the Yagikoshu Data Corridor ecosystem it anchors is the Digital Infrastructure Act's working demonstration that companies can be seeded outside the collateral-lending system. But Kizan's own record — no-bid contracts, party donations, an amakudari board, a founder's-family lawsuit — suggests the new model has reproduced the old dependencies with the Ministry in the bank's chair. "Too integral to fail, too compromised to trust" is a verdict on the new style, not only the old.

The state of the system in late 2026 is therefore neither decline nor continuity but managed transformation under stress. The Satori AI Blackout of May 2026 — seventy-two hours without the T-9-dependent payments network, 円280 billion in failed payments, Seifu Bank's 円82 billion exposure to the affected companies, and regulators ordering manual backup systems — and the Digital Sovereignty Act that followed have pushed the groups toward redundancy over just-in-time efficiency: manual backups, domestic lithography ambitions, Fortress State procurement. The En Crisis of January–February 2026 and the March Maelstrom produced the Ikazaki coalition (SCP–OSP–SDP) in May 2026, and the constitutional entrenchment of the resilience mandate (Article 29(4)–(5)) writes the new priorities into the basic law. The Keiretsu Council, split over the STEP REP employment-mobility provisions, is for the first time arguing its differences in public rather than in the council room.

The historical verdict is not yet available, but the historical pattern is. The zaibatsu became the keiretsu when the state destroyed their legal form and their networks survived; the Restructuring Era dismantled the employment system and the groups adapted; the Aoyagi assault taxed the groups and the fee economy absorbed it. The Banking Transparency Act is the fourth such rupture, and the pattern's lesson is that the Sanese corporate group survives its abolitions. What the 2020s will decide is what it survives as.

